# d3-05 — product and model selection: modality, batching and budget judgement
capability-matching|2|A team needs Claude to read text from photographs of handwritten forms.|Check whether it reads that handwriting reliably first|Assume it can, since reading text is a basic capability|Type the forms up first so that the text is clean|Use the strongest tier, since handwriting is difficult|Handwriting quality varies enormously, so a test on the actual forms is what establishes whether the task is feasible at all.|Assuming it works skips the question, typing up is the work being avoided, and a stronger tier does not guarantee legibility.
scaling-decisions|2|A team wants to try Claude on a task before committing any real effort to it.|Run it manually on a handful of their real cases|Build the full workflow and evaluate it afterwards|Read about what others have done with similar tasks|Ask the team whether they think it would work|A few real cases run by hand answers the feasibility question at almost no cost, and it uses the team's actual inputs.|Building first spends the effort being tested, reading about others uses their inputs, and asking the team collects opinion rather than evidence.
cost-latency|2|An associate's outputs are long and the readers only skim the first few lines.|Ask for shorter output, since length is being paid for|Keep the length, since more detail is always safer|Keep the length and summarise it for the readers|Move to a cheaper tier to offset the output length|Output length costs money and reader attention, and neither is being repaid when the text is skimmed.|Unread detail is not safety, summarising adds a second step, and a cheaper tier pays less for exactly the same waste.
surface-selection|2|An associate wants a colleague to run a carefully built prompt without rebuilding it.|Share it in a form the colleague can run directly|Send the colleague the prompt text to paste in|Explain to the colleague how to write it themselves|Run it for the colleague whenever they need it|Sharing the working thing is what transfers the capability rather than the instructions for recreating it.|Pasted text drifts as it is edited, teaching them to rebuild it repeats the work, and running it for them makes you the bottleneck.
model-tiers|2|A team asks whether the strongest tier is the safe default for absolutely everything.|No — it costs more without helping on easy tasks|Yes — capability is never a disadvantage in itself|Yes — provided the budget can absorb the cost|No — the strongest tier is slower on every task|Paying top rates for work a cheaper tier does equally well is waste, which is why the default should follow the task.|Capability without benefit is cost, budget headroom is not a reason to spend, and speed is not the general objection.
feature-fit|2|A team needs Claude to produce output in an identical structure every single week.|Specify the structure and check it on each run|Ask for a consistent structure in the prompt text|Reformat each week's output into the structure|Accept small variations between the weekly runs|A structure that downstream work depends on has to be both specified and verified, since generation varies between runs.|Asking for consistency does not define it, weekly reformatting is manual work, and accepting variation is what breaks the downstream use.
projects-vs-chat|2|Several people each keep their own conversation with the same set of policy documents.|Move to one shared Project everyone works from|Have each person keep their own conversation going|Have one person answer everyone else's questions|Circulate the documents so everyone has a copy|One shared Project gives everyone the same grounding and one place to maintain, rather than five diverging copies.|Separate conversations drift apart, a single answerer is a bottleneck, and circulating copies multiplies what has to be kept current.
capability-matching|2|A task needs twelve steps followed exactly, where skipping one invalidates the result.|A tier that follows instructions reliably, then verify|Any tier, since the steps are written down clearly|The cheapest tier, since each step is individually simple|The strongest tier, and no verification is then needed|Reliable instruction-following differs by tier, and where omission invalidates the result the check has to exist regardless.|Written steps are not self-enforcing, individually simple steps still get skipped, and no tier removes the need to verify a strict sequence.
scaling-decisions|2|A manual review handles fifty items a day and the new workflow will produce five hundred.|Redesign the review before the volume arrives|Keep the review and let the backlog simply build up|Remove the review, since the volume is too high|Hire more reviewers to match the new higher volume|A tenfold increase makes the existing review physically impossible, so it has to change shape before the work arrives.|A backlog defers the failure, removing the review discards the control, and hiring at ten times scale is rarely the cheapest answer.
cost-latency|2|An associate is choosing between one request covering ten documents and ten separate ones.|It depends on whether the documents interact at all|One request, since fewer requests always costs less|Ten requests, since each document gets full attention|Whichever of the two is faster to write as a prompt|Documents that must be compared belong together and independent ones do not, and that is what settles the batching.|Cost and attention arguments each hold in only one case, and ease of writing is not the criterion.
feature-fit|2|A team needs the model to work from a document they are not permitted to upload.|Check what the restriction actually covers first|Upload it anyway, since the task requires the content|Describe the document from memory in the prompt|Abandon the task, since the document cannot be used|A restriction may cover the file, the data or the whole subject, and knowing which determines whether any route exists.|Uploading breaches it, memory is unreliable and may breach it anyway, and abandoning assumes the broadest reading without checking.
surface-selection|2|A team wants stakeholders to see quarterly numbers without installing anything at all.|Give them something that opens in any web browser|Send a spreadsheet they can open in their own tools|Present the numbers to them in a meeting instead|Send a document with all the numbers written out|A browser is the one thing every stakeholder already has, which makes it the lowest-friction way to reach all of them.|A spreadsheet assumes a tool and a version, a meeting requires everyone at once, and a static document cannot be explored.
model-tiers|2|An associate is asked why their workflow uses an expensive tier and does not know.|Test the cheaper tiers and choose on the evidence|Keep the expensive tier, since the workflow works|Move to the cheapest tier and watch for problems|Explain that the tier was chosen when it was built|An unjustified choice becomes a justified one by measuring the alternatives on the actual task.|Working is not the same as being right-sized, moving to the cheapest is the same guess reversed, and explaining the history does not answer the question.
scaling-decisions|3|A pilot saved twelve hours a week across four people and forty people are next.|Expect the per-person saving to fall as it widens|Multiply the saving by ten for the forty people|Expect the same saving per person as the pilot had|Expect no saving at all until everyone is trained|Pilot participants are self-selected and better supported, so the average saving typically falls as the population widens.|Straight multiplication assumes the pilot is representative, per-person parity is the same assumption, and expecting nothing ignores a real effect.
capability-matching|3|A team's task produces occasional badly wrong answers on every tier they have tested.|Look at the task itself rather than at the tiers|Try a tier they have not yet tested on the task|Accept the failures as inherent to the task itself|Add a verification pass over every single output|A failure that survives every tier is usually about how the task is posed rather than about capability.|Another tier tests the same hypothesis again, accepting it gives up early, and verification manages a symptom without diagnosing it.
cost-latency|3|A team's spend tripled with no change in the volume of work being done.|Look at what changed in the requests themselves|Assume prices rose and budget for the new level|Move to a cheaper tier to bring the spend back down|Cap usage until the spend returns to normal levels|Constant volume with rising cost points at bigger requests or a changed configuration, and finding which is the whole fix.|Assuming a price change is untested, a tier move treats an unknown cause, and a cap hides the problem rather than solving it.
projects-vs-chat|3|A shared Project is used by twelve people and one of them keeps changing the instructions.|Agree who owns the instructions and how they change|Lock the instructions so that nobody can change them|Ask that one person to stop changing the instructions|Give each of the twelve their own copy of the Project|A shared setting needs a single agreed way of being changed, which is what stops one person's edits surprising eleven others.|Locking prevents improvement, asking one person to stop does not create a rule, and per-person copies end the sharing entirely.
feature-fit|3|An associate needs a colleague to use a result that changes as the inputs are adjusted.|Build it as something that recalculates on input|Produce a table covering the likely input values|Produce the result and rerun it whenever asked|Explain the calculation so the colleague can do it|When the point is to vary the inputs, something that recomputes is what serves the need rather than a fixed answer.|A table covers only the values you guessed, rerunning makes you the bottleneck, and explaining the method delegates the work.
model-tiers|3|A team's cheaper tier matches the expensive one on 94% of the cases they tested.|Look at what the remaining six per cent have in common|Move to the cheaper tier, since 94% is a close match|Stay on the expensive tier, since 6% is a real gap|Use the cheaper tier and check a sample of outputs|The disagreements are where the tiers actually differ, and their pattern tells you whether the cheaper tier is safe for this task.|Both tier decisions are made without looking at the failures, and sampling detects the gap rather than characterising it.
surface-selection|3|A non-technical lead wants colleagues to query a policy library without learning prompting.|Set up a Project they can simply ask questions of|Write prompts for the colleagues to copy and paste|Train the colleagues in how to write good prompts|Have the lead answer the questions on their behalf|A Project turns the library into something you ask ordinary questions of, which removes the prompting skill from the path.|Copied prompts break when the question differs, training adds a barrier before any value, and routing through the lead does not scale.
cost-latency|3|A team's internal tool calls Claude on every keystroke and users find it laggy.|Call it when the user pauses rather than on each key|Move to a faster tier so each call returns sooner|Shorten the prompt so each call is cheaper to run|Show a loading indicator so the lag feels shorter|The problem is the number of calls rather than the speed of any one, and triggering on a pause removes most of them.|A faster tier and a shorter prompt both optimise a call that should not be happening, and an indicator manages perception.
capability-matching|3|A team's task involves both mechanical extraction and difficult judgement in one prompt.|Split it so each part can be matched to a tier|Use the strongest tier, since judgement is involved|Use a cheaper tier, since extraction dominates the work|Keep it together, since splitting adds coordination|Two different demands in one prompt force a single tier choice that is wrong for one of them, and splitting removes that compromise.|Either single-tier choice overpays or underperforms on half the work, and coordination cost is usually smaller than the mismatch.
scaling-decisions|3|A team is expanding a workflow to a region with different regulations and languages.|Treat it as a new workflow to be validated afresh|Expand it, since the underlying task is the same|Expand it and translate the prompts into the language|Do not expand it, since regulations differ by region|Different rules and a different language change both what is correct and how well the model performs, which is a new validation rather than a rollout.|Task similarity hides the regulatory difference, translation addresses one of two changes, and refusing forgoes a legitimate expansion.
projects-vs-chat|3|An associate is unsure whether a document belongs in Project knowledge or one chat.|Project if several conversations will need it|Project, since knowledge is where documents belong|Attach it, since attachments are always more precise|Both, so that it is available in either place|The test is reuse: material that recurs across conversations belongs in the Project, and material for one belongs with it.|Neither blanket rule considers reuse, and doing both duplicates the content so that it competes with itself.
feature-fit|3|A team needs the model to always follow the company's approved wording for one answer.|Supply the approved wording and require it verbatim|Describe the approved position and let it phrase it|Instruct it to be careful about how it words this|Review each answer against the approved wording|Where exact wording matters, providing the text and requiring it is the only thing that reliably produces it.|A described position gets rephrased, a care instruction has no target, and review catches the problem after the fact every time.
cost-latency|3|A team's costs come mostly from a small number of unusually long documents.|Handle the long documents differently from the rest|Move everything to a cheaper tier to reduce the total|Refuse documents over a certain length in the workflow|Accept it, since long documents genuinely cost more|When a few inputs dominate the bill, they are the thing to treat specially rather than changing everything else.|A blanket tier change affects the many to fix the few, a length limit refuses valid work, and accepting it forgoes a targeted saving.
surface-selection|3|An associate with no coding background wants to automate reading and sorting emails.|Look for a route that needs no code before building|Learn enough coding to build the automation themselves|Ask a developer to build the automation for them|Continue doing it by hand, since coding is required|Many automations of this shape are available without code, and checking that first avoids both the learning and the dependency.|Learning to code is a large detour, a developer creates a dependency, and assuming code is required is the untested premise.
model-tiers|4|A team's task is limited either by the model or by the quality of the information given.|Give better information first, since it is the cheaper test|Move to a stronger tier, since it is the faster test|Improve both at once for the largest improvement|Conclude it is the model, since the prompt seems fine|Two hypotheses are separated by changing one, and improving what you supply is both cheaper and more often the actual cause.|Speed of testing is not the same as cost, changing both confounds the result, and judging the prompt by appearance is not a test.
capability-matching|4|A team's model errors turn out to be caused by inconsistent input formatting.|Standardise the inputs, since the model was not the problem|Move to a stronger tier that tolerates the variation|Add instructions describing every input format seen|Accept the errors, since input quality is outside the team|A cause outside the model is fixed outside the model, and standardising removes the variation rather than paying to absorb it.|A stronger tier pays to tolerate a fixable problem, enumerating formats breaks on the next one, and input quality is usually within reach.
cost-latency|4|A team's workflow makes three sequential calls where each depends on the one before.|Nothing can be parallelised, so shorten each call instead|Run the three calls in parallel in order to save time|Merge all three of them into a single larger call|Accept the latency, since the dependency is unavoidable|A genuine dependency chain cannot be parallelised, so the available time has to come out of each individual call.|Parallel execution is impossible when each input is the previous output, merging may lose the intermediate reasoning, and accepting it forgoes real gains.
feature-fit|4|A team must decide whether to let the model use tools or supply everything in the prompt.|Tools where the data moves, prompt where it is fixed|Tools always, since they make the model more capable|Prompt always, since it is simpler and more predictable|Both, so the model can choose whichever it needs|A tool earns its complexity when the information changes between requests, and a fixed body of material is cheaper supplied directly.|Absolute answers ignore how the data behaves, and offering both leaves an unnecessary choice inside the request.
scaling-decisions|4|A workflow will be rolled out company-wide and was validated on one team's data.|Validate on data from the teams that differ the most|Roll it out, since one team's validation is evidence|Validate on a random sample from every single team|Roll it out team by team and fix problems as found|The teams whose work differs most are where the workflow is most likely to break, which makes them the informative test.|One team's validation does not generalise, a random sample spends effort where it is least likely to find anything, and staged rollout finds problems in production.
projects-vs-chat|4|An associate wants a preference about tone to hold across every future conversation.|Put it where it applies without being restated|State the preference at the start of each conversation|Ask the model to remember it for the next time|Correct the tone whenever an answer does not match|A standing preference belongs in a setting that applies automatically, since anything requiring restatement will eventually be forgotten.|Per-conversation statement is the repetition being avoided, a request to remember does not persist, and correction never becomes a rule.
model-tiers|4|A team could spend more on a stronger tier or the same amount on better inputs.|Compare them on the same task before deciding|Spend on the tier, since capability is the harder limit|Spend on inputs, since they are within the team's control|Split the budget evenly between the two options|Both are plausible and the return on each is measurable on this task, which makes the comparison the decision.|Either preference is a guess about where the limit sits, and splitting spends half on whichever turns out not to matter.
surface-selection|4|A team wants a conversation preserved so its approach can be reused by others later.|Extract the approach into something that is reusable|Keep the conversation and share a link to it later|Copy the conversation into a shared team document|Ask the model to summarise how the work was done|A conversation records one instance, and what makes it reusable is separating the method from that instance.|A link and a copy both preserve the transcript rather than the technique, and a generated summary describes rather than packages it.
capability-matching|4|A first-pass screen flags anything needing attention and misses roughly one in fifty.|Ask what happens to the cases the screen misses|Accept it, since one in fifty is a good hit rate|Move to a stronger tier to reduce the miss rate|Have a person re-screen everything it did not flag|A screen's miss rate only matters in light of what a missed case costs, and that determines whether one in fifty is fine or unacceptable.|A hit rate without consequence is meaningless, a stronger tier improves the number without pricing it, and re-screening everything defeats the screen.
cost-latency|4|A team's workflow costs two hundred a month and saves about eight hours of work.|Compare it against what those eight hours are worth|Keep it, since eight hours clearly exceeds the cost|Drop it, since two hundred a month is a real expense|Keep it and try to reduce the monthly cost further|The comparison is between the money and the value of the time freed, and that value depends on what the eight hours now do.|Both quick verdicts assume a value for the hours, and optimising the cost skips the question of whether it is worth having.
feature-fit|4|A team needs output to arrive inside a system that accepts only one fixed layout.|Fix the layout deterministically after generation|Instruct the model to always use that exact layout|Change the receiving system to accept variation|Have a person reformat the output before it is sent|A strict downstream contract needs a deterministic step, because generated text will vary however precisely it is instructed.|Instructions are followed most of the time rather than always, changing the receiving system is a heavier fix, and a person in the path defeats the automation.
scaling-decisions|4|A team's Claude spend is growing and finance wants a figure for next year.|Give a range with the assumptions that drive it|Extrapolate this year's growth into next year|Give this year's figure, since next year is unknown|Give the highest plausible figure just to be safe|A forecast under genuine uncertainty is a range with its drivers named, which is what lets finance plan and revise it later.|Straight extrapolation assumes the growth continues, repeating this year ignores the trend, and a worst case misallocates budget.
surface-selection|4|A team must choose between a shared Project and a scheduled job for a weekly report.|Scheduled where it must happen, Project where it is asked for|Project, since the team can then ask follow-up questions|Scheduled, since a weekly report ought to be automatic|Both, so the report exists and can also be queried|The distinction is whether the report has to appear without anyone acting, and that is what separates the two surfaces.|Follow-up questions and automation are each an advantage of one, and running both maintains two things where one is needed.
